@page "/pdformmessage"
@using PanoramicData.Blazor.Models

<h1>PDFormMessage</h1>

<p>
	An inline question form for <code>PDChat</code>. One question per tab, answered in any order, one
	submit. Every question is optional, and the whole form can be dismissed - a form that will not let
	a conversation continue until it is filled in is worse than no form.
</p>

<div class="alert alert-info">
	<strong>Try it in the chat:</strong> type <code>form</code> (or <code>question</code>) into the chat
	on this page and the fake chat service will always reply with a form covering every answer kind.
	Answer some, skip some, and submit - the answers come back as an ordinary chat message, so they sit
	in the transcript in order.
</div>

<h2>Answer kinds</h2>

<p>Eight, each chosen to remove a specific way a free-text answer goes wrong.</p>

<table class="table table-sm">
	<thead>
		<tr><th style="width: 9rem;">Kind</th><th style="width: 11rem;">Renders as</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td><code>SingleChoice</code></td>
			<td>Radio buttons</td>
			<td>
				&quot;Which is your <em>favourite</em>?&quot; The choices are alternatives, so picking one rules
				out the rest. Each option carries a label and an optional description - a bare list of labels
				makes the reader guess at the trade-off, which is the thing they are being asked about.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>MultipleChoice</code></td>
			<td>Checkboxes</td>
			<td>
				&quot;Which do you <em>like</em>?&quot; A genuinely different question, kept as a separate kind
				rather than a flag so a renderer cannot show radios where checkboxes were meant. Answers are
				reported in the order the question offered them, not alphabetically.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>Scale</code></td>
			<td>Slider</td>
			<td>
				1..4, 1..5, 0..1. Name <strong>every</strong> point - &quot;Strongly disagree, Disagree, Agree,
				Strongly agree&quot; - and the answer records <em>Agree</em> rather than <em>2</em>. The numeric
				value is still kept alongside, so anything counting or averaging can. A bare &quot;3&quot; is
				meaningless a month later: three out of what, and which end is good?
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>Text</code></td>
			<td>Input or textarea</td>
			<td>
				Single or multi-line. Optionally <strong>pre-filled with a suggested draft</strong> for the user
				to edit - &quot;here is a draft, change what is wrong&quot; asks far less of someone than
				&quot;write this&quot;, and gets a better answer. Submitting the suggestion unchanged still
				counts as answered: accepting a draft is a decision, not an absence of one.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>DateTime</code></td>
			<td>Date picker</td>
			<td>
				&quot;When did this start?&quot; as free text gets &quot;last Tuesday&quot;, which then has to be
				resolved against a timezone somebody is guessing at. <code>IncludeTime</code> adds a time and is
				off by default: most questions want a day, and asking for a time the asker does not need invites
				a made-up one.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>Number</code></td>
			<td>Number input</td>
			<td>
				&quot;How many users are affected?&quot; is not a scale, because it is unbounded, and as free
				text it comes back as &quot;about twenty&quot;. Optional minimum, maximum and unit. The unit
				travels with the answer, so &quot;20&quot; is never left needing a key.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>Ranking</code></td>
			<td>Ordered list</td>
			<td>
				&quot;Put these in priority order.&quot; Different from multiple choice: it captures relative
				strength, which is what &quot;what should I do first?&quot; actually asks. Moved with up/down
				buttons rather than drag, so it stays usable by keyboard. An untouched ranking is reported as
				<em>skipped</em>, not as the order the options happened to be listed in - treating a default
				order as an answer would manufacture a preference nobody expressed.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>Acknowledgement</code></td>
			<td>Single checkbox</td>
			<td>
				&quot;I understand this will restart the service.&quot; Deliberately not a two-option choice:
				there is no &quot;No&quot; to pick, only an acknowledgement given or withheld, and withholding is
				a skip. The statement goes in the first option's label.
			</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>

<p>
	Either choice kind can offer <strong>Other</strong>, revealing a free-text box. On a multiple-choice
	question &quot;Other&quot; sits <em>alongside</em> the real selections rather than replacing them -
	someone can like vanilla, pistachio, and one more the list did not offer. On a single choice it
	clears the previous pick, so the box does not start pre-filled with an answer just rejected.
</p>

<h2>How it behaves</h2>

<ul>
	<li>
		<strong>Every question is optional, and so is the whole form.</strong> Skipping is recorded
		explicitly rather than inferred from a missing entry, so the asker can tell &quot;declined to
		answer&quot; from &quot;never asked&quot;.
	</li>
	<li>
		<strong>Submit appears only on the last tab; every other tab offers Next.</strong> With a submit
		button on every question most people press it on the first one - they have done exactly what the
		button told them to - and the asker receives a form that is one-sixth answered. Submitting early
		is still one click on the last tab, which makes it deliberate rather than accidental.
	</li>
	<li>
		<strong>Dismissing sends nothing at all.</strong> Not an empty submission - a message saying
		&quot;the user ignored your questions&quot; would invite the asker to press the point, which is
		what making the form optional was meant to avoid.
	</li>
	<li>
		<strong>The dot on a tab marks an answer, quietly.</strong> Not a tick: with every question
		optional, a tick implies the unanswered ones are wrong.
	</li>
	<li>
		<strong>Answers arrive as an ordinary chat message</strong> carrying both a readable rendering and
		the structured submission, so a consumer needs no new wiring and the transcript stays in order.
	</li>
</ul>

<h2>Standalone</h2>

<p>The component works outside a chat too. This one reports its outcome below.</p>

<div style="max-width: 620px;">
	<PDFormMessage Form="_form" OnSubmitted="OnSubmittedAsync" OnDismissed="OnDismissedAsync" />
</div>

@if (_outcome is not null)
{
	<h3 class="mt-3">Outcome</h3>
	<pre class="p-2 border rounded">@_outcome</pre>
}
